[?2004l[?2004hroot@c15b0fb7b942:/app# cat >> README.md <<'EOF'
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Resuming and stopping safely
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> `last_id` is an **exclusive** resume cursor. Each batch (or partial batch on
[?2004l[?2004h> SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP) first flushes and fsyncs its shard files and index directory,
[?2004l[?2004h> then atomically replaces and fsyncs the checkpoint. The checkpoint records each
[?2004l[?2004h> shard's committed byte length along with the cursor and document count. On restart,
[?2004l[?2004h> extra shards and bytes beyond those lengths are discarded before processing more
[?2004l[?2004h> documents. Thus a SIGKILL, including one during a write or shard rotation, can replay
[?2004l[?2004h> only uncommitted work, without duplicates or skipped committed documents. Missing
[?2004l[?2004h> committed bytes cause an error rather than silently advancing past lost data.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Checkpoints from before this protocol cannot certify their rows and trigger a
[?2004l[?2004h> one-time rebuild. The transform and JSONL row format are unchanged. A completed
[?2004l[?2004h> checkpoint makes subsequent runs no-ops (after recovery); use `./jobs clean` for a
[?2004l[?2004h> new full rebuild if the source corpus has changed. Do not run `clean` during a job.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> For an attended backfill, `ops/overrides/reindex.json` can temporarily override
[?2004l[?2004h> limits as documented in `ops/supervise.py`. Remove it afterwards to restore normal
[?2004l[?2004h> budgets and replica cooldowns. Use `./jobs history` to audit the limits actually used.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
